    <briefdescription>
<para>Reproducing kernel approximation with implicit gradients. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The shape function values are those of <ref refid="class_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_meshfree_1_1_reproducing_kernel_approximation" kindref="compound">ReproducingKernelApproximation</ref>. The gradients are not obtained by differentiating <formula id="149">$ \Psi_A $</formula> but are constructed directly as corrected kernels (implicit, or synchronized, gradients),     <formula id="150">\[  \Psi^{(i)}_A(\boldsymbol{x}) = \boldsymbol{H}^T(\boldsymbol{x} - \boldsymbol{x}_A)\,
  \boldsymbol{b}^{(i)}(\boldsymbol{x})\, \phi_A(\boldsymbol{x}),
  \qquad \boldsymbol{M}(\boldsymbol{x})\, \boldsymbol{b}^{(i)}(\boldsymbol{x}) = \boldsymbol{H}^{(i)}_0,
\]</formula> with the same moment matrix <formula id="151">$ \boldsymbol{M} $</formula>. The right-hand side  <formula id="152">$ \boldsymbol{H}^{(i)}_0 =
-\partial \boldsymbol{H}(\boldsymbol{z}) / \partial z_i |_{\boldsymbol{z} = \boldsymbol{0}} $</formula> (the sign stems from the shifted argument <formula id="153">$ \boldsymbol{z} = \boldsymbol{x} - \boldsymbol{x}_A $</formula>) is <formula id="154">$ -1 $</formula> at the position of the linear monomial <formula id="155">$ z_i $</formula> in <formula id="156">$ \boldsymbol{H} $</formula> and zero elsewhere (the position is found from the gradient of the basis at the origin, <ref refid="class_marmot_1_1_math_1_1_monomial_basis" kindref="compound">Math::MonomialBasis</ref>, so it is correct for any order). This enforces the gradient reproducing conditions    <formula id="157">\[  \sum_A \Psi^{(i)}_A(\boldsymbol{x})\, \boldsymbol{x}_A^{\boldsymbol{\alpha}} =
  \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{x}^{\boldsymbol{\alpha}}}{\partial x_i}, \qquad |\boldsymbol{\alpha}| \le n,
\]</formula> so the implicit gradients reproduce the derivatives of all polynomials up to the completeness order, but they are not the derivatives of the values <formula id="149">$ \Psi_A $</formula>. They require neither kernel gradients nor <formula id="158">$ \boldsymbol{M}_{,i} $</formula>: all <formula id="159">$ 1 + d $</formula> right-hand sides are solved with one QR decomposition of <formula id="151">$ \boldsymbol{M} $</formula>. The completeness order of a point is chosen as for <ref refid="class_marmot_1_1_discretizations_1_1_meshfree_1_1_reproducing_kernel_approximation" kindref="compound">ReproducingKernelApproximation</ref>, including the optional limit on the condition of <formula id="151">$ \boldsymbol{M} $</formula>.</para>
<para>At a point evaluated with the order 0 (too few covering kernels, or the limit on the condition), the implicit gradients would reproduce only the derivative of a constant and vanish identically: such a point would carry no strain. It gets the Shepard gradients instead, the derivatives of its values <formula id="160">$ \Psi_A = \phi_A / \sum_B \phi_B $</formula>, as with explicit gradients; only there are kernel gradients evaluated. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
